# Pin canonical text-file line endings to LF.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs. binary; `eol=lf` pins the
# canonical in-repo line ending. On a Windows checkout, files stay LF
# on disk (no autocrlf round-trip churn), eliminating the recurring
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" warnings
# and phantom "modified" churn on tool-regenerated files.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit binary types — git's auto-detection is usually correct
# but spell out the common ones to be safe.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.svg   binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.pdb   binary
*.nupkg binary
*.snupkg binary
*.zip   binary
*.tar   binary
*.gz    binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary

# --- Op-stream defence-in-depth ---
#
# A single-file LINEAR op-stream (`.roadmap/ops.jsonl`) and its signed-head sidecar
# (`.roadmap/attestations.jsonl`) are append-only. Prefer a UNION merge (keep both sides' appended
# lines) over a hard conflict, so an accidental concurrent extension INTERLEAVES rather than
# clobbers one side's ops. A union merge breaks the SHA-256 chain by construction — that is the
# point: a torn chain is DETECTED by the store's own diagnosis and repair pass, where a clobbered
# one is a silent corruption nothing would report. (Per-lane DAG streams under `.roadmap/ops/` are
# `merge=binary` instead — lane files are content-addressed and must never interleave; that rule is
# written by the store's own lane migration, not here.)
#
# This repository holds no live store of its own. The rule is here because the conformance corpus
# ships a reference store under `wire-fixtures/dispatch/reference-side/.roadmap/`, whose bytes are
# pinned and must survive a merge unaltered.
**/.roadmap/ops.jsonl          merge=union
**/.roadmap/attestations.jsonl merge=union
